---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import ReservesCalculator from '../components/reserves/ReservesCalculator';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-reserves`;

// MedicalWebPage + BreadcrumbList JSON-LD.
const medicalPageJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Latent Vitamin D Reserves Calculator',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  description:
    'Estimate your current serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentration from stored cholecalciferol using a two-compartment pharmacokinetic model with a BMI-adjusted terminal half-life.',
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: {
    '@type': 'MedicalCondition',
    name: 'Vitamin D deficiency',
    code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' },
  },
  mainContentOfPage: {
    '@type': 'WebPageElement',
    cssSelector: 'main',
  },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};

const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D Reserves', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};

const softwareJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Latent Vitamin D Reserves Calculator',
  applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web',
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: PAGE_URL,
  description:
    'Two-compartment pharmacokinetic model for estimating current serum 25(OH)D from summer sun-exposure history.',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Latent Vitamin D Reserves Calculator | Summer Storage & 25(OH)D Decay"
  description="Estimate your current serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D from stored cholecalciferol using a two-compartment pharmacokinetic model. Accounts for adipose sequestration, skin phototype, BMI, and age."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalPageJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(softwareJsonLd)} />

  <ReservesCalculator client:load />

  <!-- Long-form scientific content for SEO -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 prose prose-lg prose-amber">
    <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

    <h2 id="pharmacokinetics" class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">
      Pharmacokinetics of cholecalciferol and 25-hydroxyvitamin D
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cholecalciferol (vitamin&nbsp;D<sub>3</sub>) synthesised in the epidermis from
      7-dehydrocholesterol enters the bloodstream bound to vitamin&nbsp;D-binding protein (DBP).
      In the liver it is 25-hydroxylated by the CYP2R1 enzyme, producing 25-hydroxyvitamin&nbsp;D
      &#91;25(OH)D&#93; â€” the metabolite measured clinically because it best reflects overall
      vitamin&nbsp;D status. A second hydroxylation in the kidney (CYP27B1) yields the biologically
      active hormone 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin&nbsp;D (calcitriol), which is tightly regulated by
      parathyroid hormone, calcium, and phosphorus.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Circulating 25(OH)D has an <em>apparent</em> half-life of 15â€“25 days when studied in
      short-duration tracer experiments (Jones 2008; Vieth 1999). However, the whole-body
      terminal half-life is considerably longer â€” around 60 days in lean adults and 80â€“90+ days
      in obese adults â€” because cholecalciferol partitioned into adipose tissue re-enters the
      circulation over months. This adipose sequestration explains why obese individuals often
      show lower serum 25(OH)D at any given UVB dose (Wortsman 2000; Drincic 2012), and why
      winter decline curves in temperate populations follow an exponential trajectory with a
      time constant of roughly two months (Kimlin 2007; Webb 2010).
    </p>

    <h2 id="model" class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">
      The two-compartment decay model used here
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The calculator assumes a first-order elimination process with an effective terminal
      half-life scaled to body-mass index. Peak summer serum 25(OH)D is estimated from a
      pigmentation-, age-, and dose-adjusted anchor point (Fitzpatrick II, 90 min of near-noon
      UVB per week, exposed fraction â‰ˆ 0.28 â†’ 40 ng/mL, per Holick 2007). The model then
      projects forward from your entered last-effective-exposure date using
      <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">C(t) = C<sub>peak</sub>Â·e<sup>âˆ’Î»t</sup></code>
      where <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">Î» = ln(2)/tÂ½</code>. Daily supplemental
      cholecalciferol asymptotically raises steady-state serum 25(OH)D by ~1 ng/mL per 100 IU/day
      in lean adults (Heaney 2003), and this contribution is added with the same time constant.
    </p>

    <h2 id="thresholds" class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">
      Clinical thresholds
    </h2>
    <ul class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li><strong>&lt; 12 ng/mL (30 nmol/L)</strong> â€” severe deficiency; risk of rickets in
        children and osteomalacia in adults.</li>
      <li><strong>12â€“20 ng/mL</strong> â€” deficient by the IOM 2011 threshold; associated with
        secondary hyperparathyroidism.</li>
      <li><strong>20â€“30 ng/mL</strong> â€” insufficient by the Endocrine Society 2011 threshold;
        may not fully optimise musculoskeletal outcomes.</li>
      <li><strong>30â€“50 ng/mL</strong> â€” the range most guidelines consider sufficient for
        general population health.</li>
      <li><strong>&gt; 100 ng/mL</strong> â€” potential toxicity; hypercalcaemia possible with
        sustained levels above ~150 ng/mL.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="limitations" class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">
      Limitations
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      This is a population-level model and cannot capture individual variability in DBP
      polymorphisms, CYP2R1 activity, renal function, malabsorption, or medication interactions
      (e.g., glucocorticoids and anticonvulsants can accelerate 25(OH)D catabolism). If your
      estimate suggests insufficiency and you have symptoms â€” bone pain, unexplained fatigue,
      frequent infections â€” please seek a serum 25(OH)D measurement from a healthcare provider
      rather than relying on a modelled projection.
    </p>

    <h2 id="citations" class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">
      Key citations
    </h2>
    <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>Holick MF. Vitamin D deficiency. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2007;357(3):266â€“281.</li>
      <li>Holick MF et al. Evaluation, treatment, and prevention of vitamin D deficiency:
        Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline. <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em>.
        2011;96(7):1911â€“1930.</li>
      <li>Institute of Medicine. Dietary Reference Intakes for Calcium and Vitamin D. National
        Academies Press; 2011.</li>
      <li>Wortsman J et al. Decreased bioavailability of vitamin D in obesity. <em>Am J Clin
        Nutr</em>. 2000;72(3):690â€“693.</li>
      <li>Drincic AT et al. Volumetric dilution, rather than sequestration, best explains the
        low vitamin D status of obesity. <em>Obesity (Silver Spring)</em>. 2012;20(7):1444â€“1448.</li>
      <li>Jones G. Pharmacokinetics of vitamin D toxicity. <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>.
        2008;88(2):582Sâ€“586S.</li>
      <li>Vieth R. Vitamin D supplementation, 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentrations, and safety.
        <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>. 1999;69(5):842â€“856.</li>
      <li>Heaney RP et al. Human serum 25-hydroxycholecalciferol response to extended oral
        dosing with cholecalciferol. <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>. 2003;77(1):204â€“210.</li>
      <li>Kimlin MG. Geographic location and vitamin D synthesis. <em>Mol Aspects Med</em>.
        2008;29(6):453â€“461.</li>
      <li>Webb AR et al. The role of sunlight exposure in determining the vitamin D status of
        the UK white adult population. <em>Br J Dermatol</em>. 2010;163(5):1050â€“1055.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-reserves" />
</BaseLayout>
